"Public Service Media - Freedom of Expression (https://www.coe.int/en/web/freedom-expression/public-service-media)\nciteturn43view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.coe.int/en/web/freedom-expression/public-service-media\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 148\nL0: # Navigation\nL1: \nL2:   * 【0†Skip to Content】\nL3: \nL4: Freedom of Expression\nL5:   1. 【1†Home】\nL6:   2. 【2†Media】\nL7:     1. 【3†Freedom of expression in times of conflict】\nL8:     2. 【4†Media in times of health crises】\nL9:     3. 【5†Defamation】\nL10:     4. 【6†Gender and Media】\nL11:     5. 【7†Hate Speech】\nL12:     6. 【8†Information Disorder】\nL13:     7. 【9†Media and Refugees】\nL14:     8. 【10†Media Regulatory Authorities】\nL15:     9. 【11†Public Service Media】\nL16:     10. 【12†Safety of Journalists】\nL17:       1. 【13†Implementation CM/Rec(2016)4】\nL18:     11. 【14†Community media】\nL19:     12. 【15†Media Literacy】\nL20:   3. 【16†Internet Governance】\nL21:     1. 【17†Standard Setting】\nL22:     2. 【18†International Dialogue】\nL23:       1. 【19†EuroDIG】\nL24:       2. 【20†Internet Governance Forum】\nL25:       3. 【21†ICANN】\nL26:     3. 【22†Council of Europe Strategy】\nL27:     4. 【23†Digital Societies and Media】\nL28:     5. 【24†Freedom of Expression Online】\nL29:     6. 【25†User Rights】\nL30:       1. 【26†Guide to Human Rights for Internet Users】\nL31:     7. 【27†Internet Intermediaries】\nL32:     8. 【28†Algorithms and AI development】\nL33:     9. 【29†Digital Partnership】\nL34:   4. 【30†Cooperation projects】\nL35:   5. 【31†CDMSI and expert committees】\nL36:     1. 【32†CDMSI Plenary Meetings】\nL37:     2. 【33†CDMSI Bureau meetings】\nL38:     3. 【34†Committee of Experts on Safeguarding Media Pluralism in the Online Environment (MSI-ePLU)】\nL39:     4. 【35†Committee of Experts on Media Regulators in a Platform-Based Environment (MSI-eREG)】\nL40:     5. 【36†Former Committees】\nL41:   6. 【37†Campaign for the Safety of Journalists】\nL42:     1. 【38†About the campaign】\nL43:     2. 【39†Events and Activities】\nL44:     3. 【40†National Chapters】\nL45:       1. 【41†National focal points】\nL46:       2. 【42†National committees】\nL47:       3. 【43†Repository of National Action Plans】\nL48:     4. 【44†National Initiatives on Safety of Journalists】\nL49:     5. 【45†Visibility kit】\nL50: # Public Service Media\nL51: \nL52: 【46†Image】\nL53: \nL54: The Council of Europe recognises the important role of public service media in upholding the fundamental right to freedom of expression and information, in accordance with Article 10 of the European Convention on Human Rights, enabling people to seek and receive information, and promoting the values of democracy, diversity and social cohesion.\nL55: By virtue of their remit, public service media are an important public source of unbiased information and diverse political opinions. They are particularly suited to foster pluralism and awareness of diverse opinions, notably by providing different groups in society with an opportunity to receive and impart information, to express themselves and to exchange ideas. They can contribute greatly to the promotion of social cohesion, cultural diversity and pluralist communication accessible to everyone.\nL56: In exercising their role, public service media face a number of challenges, such as securing the right level of independence from those holding economic and political power, securing appropriate funding, adapting to the digital age and maintaining high editorial standards in a competitive market.\nL57: \nL58: Thematic Leaflet\nL59: \nL60: 【47†Brief overview of the latest Council of Europe guidelines and activities to support public service media†rm.coe.int】\nL61: \nL62: Identifying Risks\nL63: In exercising their role, public service media face a number of challenges, such as securing the right level of independence from those holding economic and political power, securing appropriate funding, adapting to the digital age and maintaining high editorial standards in a competitive market.\nL64: #### 【48†Report of the Secretary General of the Council of Europe†edoc.coe.int】\nL65: \nL66: ###### Read more\nL67: \nL68: 【49†Image†static.coe.int】 In his report “【48†State of Democracy, Human Rights and the Rule of Law†edoc.coe.int】”, the Secretary General of the Council of Europe stresses that: “The year 2016 witnessed interference of governments in the appointment and dismissal procedures of members of public-service media boards.”\nL69: #### 【50†Report on attacks against journalists and media freedom in Europe†semantic-pace.net】\nL70: ###### Read more\nL71: 【49†Image†static.coe.int】 In January 2017 the Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe examined a 【51†Report on attacks against journalists and media freedom in Europe†semantic-pace.net】. Noting that the situation of public service broadcasting is difficult in several member states, the Assembly recalled that the independence of PSM from governments has to be ensured through law and practice.\nL72: It further stated that governments and parliaments must not interfere in the daily management and editorial work of such broadcasters, which should establish in-house codes of conduct for journalistic work and editorial independence from political sides.\nL73: #### 【52†Platform for the Protection of Journalism and Safety of Journalists】\nL74: ###### Read more\nL75: \nL76: 【53†Image†static.coe.int】 An emerging trend of threats to the independence of public broadcasters is revealed by the 【52†Platform for the Protection of Journalism and Safety of Journalists】 launched by the Council of Europe in April 2015. A growing number of alerts concern political interference in the editorial line of public broadcasters, insufficient safeguards in the legislation against political bias, or the lack of appropriate funding.\nL77: \nL78: Enhancing co-operation\nL79: 【54†Image†static.coe.int】 The 【55†International Conference on Public Service Media and Democracy】 (Prague, November 2016) discussed the role of parliaments in protecting the media, public service media governance and its impact on society.\nL80: \nL81: Building Capacity in Member States\nL82: Over the past decade, the Council of Europe Cooperation Programme has been implemented through 20 projects in member states and partner countries promoting media freedom, including independence and efficiency of public service media.\nL83: \nL84: 【56†Image: Freedom of the Media & Public Broadcasting in Ukraine】\nL85: #### 【57†Freedom of the Media & Public Broadcasting in Ukraine】\nL86: \nL87: The project is aimed at consolidating public broadcasting system in Ukraine, strengthening the institutional capacity of the public broadcaster’s Supervisory Board, enabling the production of higher-quality programming, developing a model of regional public broadcaster etc.\nL88: \nL89: 【58†Image: Media Freedom & Pluralism in Republic of Moldova】\nL90: #### 【59†Media Freedom & Pluralism in Republic of Moldova】\nL91: \nL92: The Project focuses on ensuring the functioning of the public service broadcasters supervisory bodies, improving their editorial policies and the existing TV and radio programs and setting up a Training Centre at Teleradio-Moldova.\nL93: \nL94: News - Public Service Media\nL95: \nL96:   * Strasbourg, France 29 September 2026\nL97: \nL98: 29-Sep-2026\nL99: ### 【60†Council of Europe Consults Experts on the Development of a Practical Guide on Independent, Sustainable and Pluralistic Public Service Media】\nL100: \nL101: 【61†View all News - Public Service Media】\nL102: \nL103: Promoting Standards\nL104: \nL105: #### 【62†The Committee of Ministers Recommendation on public service media governance (2012)】\nL106: ###### Read more\nL107: The 【62†Committee of Ministers Recommendation on public service media governance (2012)】 calls on member states to ensure that external governance arrangements for public service media guarantee the independence and sustainable development of public service media. It also set up criteria relating to a proper and well-functioning internal governance system of these organisations.\nL108: These criteria relate to the principles of independence, accountability, effective management, transparency and openness as well as responsiveness and responsibility.\nL109: Other normative documents adopted by the Committee of Ministers or the Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe promote specific aspects relevant to public service media such as:\nL110: \nL111: 【63†Image†static.coe.int】 【64†Recommendation on media and the promotion of a culture of tolerance (1997)】\nL112: \nL113: 【63†Image†static.coe.int】 【65†Recommendation on hate speech (1997) 】\nL114: \nL115: 【63†Image†static.coe.int】 【66†Recommendation on the remit of public service media in the information society (2007) 】\nL116: 【63†Image†static.coe.int】 【67†Recommendation on media pluralism and diversity of media content (2007) 】\nL117: \nL118: 【63†Image†static.coe.int】 【68†Resolution on indicators for media in a democracy (2008) †assembly.coe.int】\nL119: \nL120: 【63†Image†static.coe.int】 【69†Recommendation on the funding of public service broadcasting (2009) 】\nL121: \nL122: 【70† Recommendation CM/Rec(2022)16 on combating hate speech†search.coe.int】\nL123: \nL124: 【71†Image】\nL125: ##### \"Everyone has the right to freedom of expression\"\nL126: \nL127: Art. 10 European Convention on Human Rights\nL128: \nL129: Resources\nL130: \nL131: 【72† Publications】\nL132: \nL133: 【73† Reports】\nL134: \nL135: 【74† Adopted texts】\nL136: \nL137: 【75† Leaflets】\nL138: \nL139: 【76† Videos】\nL140: \nL141: 【77†Image†static.coe.int】 【78†Useful links】\nL142: \nL143: follow us\nL144: \nL145: \nL146: \nL147: #### 【79†Image†static.coe.int】 【80†Contact us】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npardon | Wex | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute (https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/pardon)\nciteturn43view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/pardon\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 28\nL0: Please help us improve our site!\nL1: \nL2: 【0†No thank you】\nL3: # pardon\nL4: A pardon is the use of executive power that exempts the individual to whom it was given from punishment. The president’s pardon power is based on 【1†Article II】 of the Constitution which says, “…he shall have power to grant reprieves and pardons for offenses against the United States, except in cases of impeachment. Unlike a 【2†commutation】, which shortens or eliminates an individual’s punishment, a pardon absolves the individual of guilt.\nL5: For example, President Trump commuted Roger Stone’s prison sentence so that Mr. Stone did not serve the punishment for his guilty conduct. On the other hand, President John F. Kennedy pardoned all first time offenders convicted of crimes under the Narcotics Control Act of 1956 to signal to Congress that the law needed to be changed.\nL6: [Last reviewed in August of 2020 by the 【3†Wex Definitions Team†about.law.cornell.edu】]\nL7: \nL8: Wex\nL9: \nL10:   * 【4†CIVICS】\nL11:   * 【5†THE LEGAL PROCESS】\nL12:   * 【6†criminal procedure】\nL13:   * 【7†wex definitions】\nL14:   * 【8†government】\nL15: ## Wex Toolbox\nL16: \nL17: * * *\nL18: \nL19: * * *\nL20: \nL21:   * 【9†Accessibility】\nL22:   * 【10†About LII】\nL23:   * 【11†Contact us】\nL24:   * 【12†Advertise here】\nL25:   * 【13†Help】\nL26:   * 【14†Terms of use】\nL27:   * 【15†Privacy】\n"
